.TH CHTTP_RUN_QUERY 3 "2026-07-14" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttp_run_query \- run one request with explicit headers, with the default client
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpclient.h>

ccol_retval_t chttp_run_query(chttp_method_t method, const char *url,
                              const chttp_request_body_t *body,
                              chmap headers,
                              chttpcli_response **resp_out);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This is a short wrapper. It builds a chttp_request_t inside. It
attaches headers to that request without a transfer of ownership. It
then calls chttp_do and frees the request object that it built. Among
the short functions for the default client, this is the only one that
takes a header set of your choice in one call. With chttp_get,
chttp_post and the others you must build a chttp_request_t by hand
instead. That means a call to chttp_request_new and then one call to
chttp_request_set_header for each header.

The caller keeps the full ownership of headers. The function only
borrows headers for the time of the call. It never frees headers and
never changes headers.

The keys in headers can use any case, for example "Host",
"Content-Type" or "Accept". This module adds a default value for some
keys of its own. Those keys are host, accept, user-agent,
content-length, content-type, authorization and expect. The module
matches each of them against headers without regard to case. A header
of yours with a natural case therefore stops the matching default of
the module. The two do not both go onto the wire.

A chmap matches a key by its exact bytes. headers can therefore hold
two keys that differ as bytes and that are the same header name under
the case-insensitive rules of HTTP. "Host" and "host" together are
such a pair. chttp_request_set_header makes every key lowercase before
it stores that key, so it can never make this situation. A headers map
that you build directly gives no such guarantee. Only one key of such
a pair reaches the wire, and never both. The key that went into
headers last wins. This matches the behaviour of an update with one
exact key. The client drops the other key, and does not send it as a
second header line.

You give headers to this function directly, and it does not go through
the validation of chttp_request_set_header. No key and no value in it
may hold a CR byte or an LF byte inside it. This is the same rule as
in chttp_request_set_header, and it has the same reason. Without it,
data with a CRLF in it can inject extra header lines. Such data can
also cut the request into two. The function gives
ccol_invalid_args for such a
headers map, before it opens any connection. headers must also hold no
"Transfer-Encoding" key, in any case. The reason is the reason that
chttp_request_set_header rejects that name. This client never applies
a transfer coding to a request body, so it cannot obey such a header.
On the wire, that header sits beside the automatic Content-Length of
this module, over a body with no transfer coding. That framing is
ambiguous.

The function DOES accept a "Content-Length" key in headers. But it
checks that key on a request that carries a body (POST, PUT or PATCH).
A good value is a plain unsigned string of decimal digits. It has no
leading '+' or '-', and no whitespace inside it. It must also match
the real length of the body that the client sends. The function gives
ccol_invalid_args for a bad value, once the request reaches the wire.
See chttpclient_do(3) and chttp_request_set_header(3) for the same
check and for the same reason: the declared framing and the wire must
not disagree.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
method     The HTTP method.
url        The target URL. It must not be NULL.
body       The request body, or NULL for a request with no body.
headers    A chmap(char* -> char*) of the request headers, or NULL for
           no headers. The function borrows it for the time of the
           call. It does not consume it and does not free it. No key
           and no value may hold a CR byte or an LF byte. No key may
           be "Transfer-Encoding".
resp_out   It must not be NULL. The function sets it to NULL at once.
           If the call succeeds, it then gets the pointer to the
           response.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
The same codes as chttpclient_do. The function gives
ccol_not_enough_memory if the library cannot allocate the internal
request object. It gives ccol_invalid_args if url or resp_out is NULL.
It also gives ccol_invalid_args if headers holds a key or a value
with a CR byte or an LF byte in it. It gives the same code if
headers holds a "Transfer-Encoding" key.
.fi
